{"qid":"Q207162","names":["Kingdom of France","Bourbon Kingdom of France"],"title":"Kingdom of France","endonym":{"native":"Royaume de France","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of France grew from the Frankish domains assigned to the West Frankish kings under the Treaty of Verdun (843) and is conventionally dated as a distinct entity from the Capetian accession of Hugh Capet in 987. Under Philip II Augustus (r. 1180–1223) the crown systematically absorbed Angevin territories in northern France; Louis IX (St. Louis, r. 1226–1270) consolidated royal authority and promoted codified law, while Louis XIV (r. 1643–1715) extended France to its 'natural frontiers' and made it the dominant continental power. The kingdom ended with the proclamation of the First Republic on 21 September 1792, following Louis XVI's arrest after the storming of the Tuileries on 10 August; it was briefly restored under the Bourbons (1814–1830) but the dataset attests to the terminal date of 1792.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core borders are well documented by treaties from the 13th century onward; earlier Capetian and late Carolingian frontier zones in the east and south are reconstructed from feudal records and are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of France","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_France"},{"label":"Britannica: France — History","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/France/History"},{"label":"Britannica: Philip II (king of France)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Philip-II-king-of-France"},{"label":"Britannica: Louis XIV","url":"https://www.britannica.c